Use this tool to add a custom domain (e.g. pages.example.com) to a Typeform account.

## What this tool does
- Normalises the hostname (lowercase, no trailing dot) and validates it.
- Creates the domain in unverified status and returns the DNS records the customer must configure.
- Idempotent: when the account already owns the hostname, the existing domain is returned with created=false.
- Fails with DOMAIN_ALREADY_IN_USE when another account or product already uses the hostname.
- Does not buy, check availability of, or price domains; the customer must already own the hostname.

## Inputs
- account_id: The Typeform account id (ULID) the request operates on. Must be an account the caller is a member of. Use the list_accounts tool to discover it.
- product: pages. forms is reserved and not supported yet.
- hostname: the fully qualified hostname to add, without scheme or path (e.g. pages.example.com).

## Output
- domain: the created or reused domain, with:
  - product, domain_id, hostname
  - status: unverified or verified
  - setup.dns_records: the DNS records to configure (type, name, value)
  - created_at, updated_at
- created: true when a new domain was created, false when an existing one was reused.

## Custom-domain workflow
Public MCP callers use the public_ variant of each tool named below.
1. create_domain with the customer's hostname (idempotent: an existing domain on the account is reused).
2. Ask the customer to add the returned setup.dns_records at their DNS provider. When setup.automatic_setup is present, call
   create_domain_setup_link instead to give the customer a link that configures DNS automatically.
3. revalidate_domain once DNS is in place; status becomes verified when the records resolve.
4. get_domain_status or list_domains to check progress. delete_domain removes a domain.


```ts
mcp__Typeform__accounts_public_create_domain(args: // PublicCreateDomainToolInput
{
// Typeform account id (ULID) to operate on; the caller must be a member. See the list_accounts tool.
account_id: string,
// Fully qualified hostname without scheme or path, e.g. pages.example.com.
hostname: string,
// DomainProduct
//
// Product the domain belongs to.
product: "pages" | "forms",
}): Promise<// CreateDomainToolOutput
{
// True when a new domain was created, false when an existing one was reused.
created: boolean,
// DomainRecord
//
// A custom domain.
domain: {
// Creation timestamp (RFC 3339).
created_at: string, // format: "date-time"
// Unique id of the domain; pass it to the other domain tools.
domain_id: string,
// Normalised hostname, e.g. pages.example.com.
hostname: string,
// DomainProduct
//
// Product the domain belongs to.
product: "pages" | "forms",
// DomainSetup
//
// What the customer must do to point the domain at Typeform.
setup: {
// AutomaticSetupHint
//
// Present when the customer can configure DNS automatically instead of adding the DNS records by hand. Call the named tool to get the setup link.
automatic_setup?: {
// True when automatic setup can be used for this domain.
available: boolean,
// Name of the tool that returns the automatic setup link.
tool: string,
},
// DNS records the customer must configure at their DNS provider.
dns_records: Array<
// DomainDNSRecord
//
// A DNS record the customer must configure at their DNS provider.
{
// Record name (host) to configure.
name: string,
// DNS record type, e.g. CNAME.
type: string,
// Record value (target) to configure.
value: string,
}
>,
},
// DomainStatus
//
// Verification status of the domain.
status: "unverified" | "verified",
// Last update timestamp (RFC 3339).
updated_at: string, // format: "date-time"
},
}>;
```

Use this tool to get a link the customer follows to configure DNS for a custom domain automatically,
instead of adding DNS records by hand.

## What this tool does
- Returns a short-lived link to a guided flow that configures the domain's DNS records at the customer's DNS provider.
- Every call issues a new link: request a fresh one if the customer reports the link expired.
- Does not change the domain's status; call revalidate_domain after the customer has completed the flow.
- Only available when the domain's setup.automatic_setup is present; otherwise the customer must add setup.dns_records manually.

## Inputs
- product: pages. forms is reserved and not supported yet.
- domain_id: id of the domain, as returned by list_domains or create_domain.

## Output
- product, domain_id: the domain the link was issued for.
- provider: the automatic DNS setup provider that issued the link.
- url: the link to give to the customer. Present it to the customer and ask them to open it and complete the flow.

## Custom-domain workflow
Public MCP callers use the public_ variant of each tool named below.
1. create_domain with the customer's hostname (idempotent: an existing domain on the account is reused).
2. Ask the customer to add the returned setup.dns_records at their DNS provider. When setup.automatic_setup is present, call
   create_domain_setup_link instead to give the customer a link that configures DNS automatically.
3. revalidate_domain once DNS is in place; status becomes verified when the records resolve.
4. get_domain_status or list_domains to check progress. delete_domain removes a domain.


```ts
