Return the capability matrix for the Typeform form editing tools.

Call once before authoring any ops, and use the response instead of guessing field types, op names, or validation keys.

### Response fields
- supported_types: field types accepted by forms-public_patch_form
- container_types: types that accept nested fields. Containers have no settable property keys
  and are absent from property_keys_by_type. Manage children via add_field (parent_ref = the
  container's ref), move_field (move_to.parent_ref moves into the container, null moves to the
  top level; before_ref alone reorders within the current scope) and delete_field, not a properties bag.
- choice_bearing_types / choices_required_on_add: types that carry or require choices
- validation_keys_by_type: accepted validation keys per field type. Write them under
  ops[].properties.validations.
- property_keys_by_type: accepted property keys per field type. Write them, with title, type
  and choices, under ops[].properties, never at the op's top level.
- settings_keys: every key accepted in ops[].settings of the update_settings op, with its builder
  label and allowed values. Form settings (form mode, quiz answer feedback, display options,
  signed-form email notifications, language, system messages) change with that op, not field ops.
  Not available: the builder's 'Send email for new responses' toggle and respondent notification emails.
- ending_property_keys_by_type: ending types add_ending can create (screen_properties.type) and
  the screen_properties keys each accepts. "url_redirect" is the builder's "Redirect to URL" ending:
  respondents are sent straight to redirect_url instead of seeing a screen. It differs from
  button_mode "redirect", which adds a button to a thank-you screen.
  redirect_url may embed {{field:<field ref>}}, {{var:<name>}} and {{hidden:<name>}}: refs come from
  forms-public_get_form, names from "variables" and "hidden" in its full view.
- patch_ops: op verbs grouped by domain (field / logic). A rule holds at most one hide:
  if it has one, widen its condition with update_logic_rule instead of appending another.
- condition_ops_by_type: valid ops per field type, keyed on the CONDITION's field type
  (condition.field_ref if set, else trigger_field_ref). Use this to author logic rules.
- compound_conditions: whether condition.op can be "and"/"or" (compound_conditions.ops)
  with condition.conditions holding leaf conditions, and how deep nesting can go
  (compound_conditions.max_depth — a leaf cannot itself be "and"/"or"). Orthogonal to
  condition_ops_by_type: it applies at the rule level, not per field type.
- logic_actions: accepted action types for logic rules
- error_codes / side_effect_codes / concurrency_codes: with recovery hints

```ts
mcp__Typeform__forms_public_get_capabilities(args: object): Promise<{
// Block types that may carry choices.
choice_bearing_types: string[],
// Block types that must be created with at least one choice.
choices_required_on_add: string[],
// Whether condition.op can be "and"/"or" with condition.conditions holding leaf conditions, and how deep nesting can go. Orthogonal to condition_ops_by_type — this applies at the rule level (any logic-capable trigger type), not per field type.
compound_conditions: {
// How many levels of and/or nesting are allowed. A leaf inside conditions cannot itself be and/or beyond this depth.
max_depth: integer,
// The compound condition operators — currently and, or.
ops: string[],
// Whether compound (and/or) conditions are supported.
supported: boolean,
},
// Optimistic-concurrency codes with recovery hints.
concurrency_codes: { [key: string]: string },
// Valid condition ops per field type, keyed on the CONDITION's field type (condition.field_ref if set, else trigger_field_ref). Use this to author logic rules — ops are type-dependent (e.g. choice fields use is/is_not only).
condition_ops_by_type: { [key: string]: string[] },
// Block types that accept child fields. Manage children with add_field (parent_ref set to this block's ref), move_field (move_to.parent_ref to move into or out of the container, before_ref to reorder), and delete_field. There is no fields property to set directly.
container_types: string[],
// Ending types add_ending can create (screen_properties.type), each mapped to the screen_properties keys it accepts.
ending_property_keys_by_type: { [key: string]: string[] },
// Patch error codes with recovery hints.
error_codes: { [key: string]: string },
// Accepted action types for logic rules.
logic_actions: string[],
// Maximum number of choices per block type. Types absent from the map have no limit.
max_choices_by_type: { [key: string]: integer },
// Patch operation verbs grouped by domain.
patch_ops: { [key: string]: string[] },
// Per-type map of settable property keys (e.g. steps, shape, description).
property_keys_by_type: { [key: string]: string[] },
// Every key accepted in ops[].settings of the update_settings op, with its builder label and allowed values.
settings_keys: { [key: string]: string },
// Side-effect codes with hints. Any side effect returned by validate_patch warrants warning the user before commit.
side_effect_codes: { [key: string]: string },
// All block types accepted by patch_form, sorted.
supported_types: string[],
// All known validation keys with prose descriptions.
validation_keys: { [key: string]: string },
// Per-type map of accepted validation keys.
validation_keys_by_type: { [key: string]: string[] },
}>;
```

Retrieve a form. Always call before forms-public_validate_patch so your ops are authored against the current state.

## Prerequisites
- id: Required. Call forms-public_list_forms to find it, or use the id returned by forms-public_create_form.
- account_id: Required. Call accounts-list_accounts to obtain this value if not already known.

### Parameters
- account_id: Account ID (required)
- id: The form ID (required)
- view: one of
  - "skeleton": refs, types, titles, welcome/thankyou screens, and hidden (form-level hidden field names).
    Container fields include nested children inline. Cheapest read; start here.
  - "fields": full per-field detail for specific refs (requires refs, max 50, any depth). Also returns a "logic" array with the rules
    those fields trigger, and a "missing" array for refs not found.
    To edit a field, send its choices as {ref, label} and put title, type and validations inside properties.
    Required before writing logic against choice fields: choice conditions need choice REFs, which only this view exposes.
  - "full" (default): complete form including settings, theme, and logic. Most expensive; use sparingly.
- refs: list of field refs (only used when view="fields"). Max 50 per call.

Note: field refs may be rewritten to UUIDs when a form is published. Re-read the form rather than reusing refs authored before a publish.

```ts
mcp__Typeform__forms_public_get_form(args: {
// Account ID (required)
account_id: string,
// Form ID (required)
id: string,
// Field refs to drill into (used only when view=fields). Max 50 per call.
refs: string[] | null,
// skeleton: compact map of the form — id, title, and each field's ref/type/title/required/choice_count/rule_count. Container fields include their nested children inline under `fields`, so the agent sees nested refs without drilling. rule_count shows how many logic rules are triggered by that field (omitted when 0). Also lists the form-level hidden field names under `hidden`. Drops property bags, choice labels, theme, settings, and layout.
// fields: drill-down. Requires `refs`. Returns the same per-field shape as `full` but only for the requested refs, plus a `logic` array with the rules those fields trigger and a `missing` array for refs not found.
// full (default): complete form including settings, theme, logic.
view: "skeleton" | "fields" | "full" | null,
}): Promise<{
// Hypermedia links
_links?: object,
// Creation timestamp
created_at?: string,
// Form fields
fields?: any[],
// Hidden fields
hidden?: string[],
// Form ID
id: string,
// Last update timestamp
last_updated_at?: string,
// Form logic rules. With view=fields, only the rules triggered by the requested refs.
logic?: any[],
// Form settings
