## What this tool does
Computes measures bucketed over time for a single field or an entire dataset.

Call insights-public_discover first to resolve form_id / audience_id / study_id / field_id / property_id and to
learn each field's filter_type, filter_operators, and filter_values before filtering.
For a study, field_id is the question's ref, and task_ref with field_id null buckets a usability task's matrix.
Screener questions' counts include participants who were screened out. New sessions appear after a short delay.

## Use when
- User asks how responses, counts, scores, or averages changed over time
- User wants a trend, chart, or time series of any metric
- User asks about monthly/weekly/daily breakdowns of responses or scores

## Also known as
trend, time series, time-series, over time, by month, by week, by day, timeline, chart data, historical data

## Constraints
- Provide exactly one of form_id, audience_id or study_id — never more than one, never none. task_ref needs study_id.
- Optional parameters may be omitted or sent as null.
- variable_name needs study_id and field_id; with it use dimension answer_choice_label and measure count for per-category counts per bucket.

## Reading the response
- The response is sparse: buckets with no data are omitted from data.
- time_range echoes the window the query actually covered. When start is omitted it is derived from
  the form's, audience's or study's creation date, so always read the window from the response, not the request.
- A bucket inside time_range but absent from data collected zero — it is not missing data. An empty
  data array means zero across the whole of time_range.
- The response does not say whether the form was accepting responses during a given bucket, so a zero
  bucket alone does not distinguish "nobody responded" from "the form was not collecting".

## Frequency analysis (studies)
- An open-ended study question is analysed through its categorical variables: pass study_id, field_id and variable_name (one of the field's variables in insights-public_discover).
